WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.670
Speaker 1: It's great to have back on the
program once again, Anand Giridharadas, who

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.649
is a journalist and commentator.

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:13.620
The new book is The Persuaders at the Frontlines
of the Fight for Hearts, Minds and Democracy.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.980
Really appreciate having you back on.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.400
Speaker 4: Thank you for having me back.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:24.449
Speaker 1: So intuitively, I have the sense
that on political issues, it is now more difficult

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:28.030
to persuade people even when the facts are
on one side.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:33.950
And I think maybe the first place to start
is, is that perception accurate?

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:39.160
Is it true that there we are in an era where
political beliefs are in a sense, more like

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.550
sticky or stubborn or difficult to change?

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:46.450
Speaker 4: Yeah, well, I think another way
to think about that is, is the is the fraction

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:51.559
of people who are very difficult to persuade,
has that fraction of people grown.

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:56.030
I think that's in a way what we mean when
we talk about polarization.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.030
Right.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:03.920
That that the country has sorted into these
kind of two political tribes.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:10.240
But I think part of where the Persuaders book
starts is from the premise that we have confused

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:17.210
polarization for meaning that no one is persuadable
anymore, which is not true.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:24.030
It is the case that significant minorities
of people on each side are not persuadable.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:29.360
And what defines that group on each side,
in the left and the right is not how strong

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.240
their opinions are, but how deeply held those
strong opinions are.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.260
Which is to say you can imagine someone we
all know, this person who will huff and puff

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.030
about a strong border.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.140
Right.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.229
But you could imagine someone who huffs and
puffs about a strong border who has watched.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:54.061
80 documentaries about immigration policy
and reads books about it and goes to, you

00:01:54.061 --> 00:01:58.530
know, militia men meet ups where they talk
about the border.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.560
That is not a persuadable voter.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.750
That is someone who not only has a strong
opinion, but as a strongly held strong opinion.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:10.470
You know, that person has talking points to
rebut any objection you might make the person

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.470
you know.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:12.470
And that exists on the left.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:17.330
Also, there's people who, you know, have a
strong stance on universal health care because

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.530
they've really they thought it's part of a
worldview.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.580
They thought about it, they bolstered it.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.690
It's part of their community consciousness.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:32.090
But then there are other people who vote alongside
the strong border guy or the universal health

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:41.590
care person who may have that opinion on the
surface but have much shallower roots of that

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:42.590
opinion.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.099
They haven't watched a bunch of documentaries
about border policy.

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:48.880
They have not read a bunch of books.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.680
They have what I think the corporate world
is sometimes called strong opinions, lightly

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:53.780
held.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.880
Right.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.780
And persuadable voters are people who might
have strong opinions but also have them lightly

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.780
held.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:02.780
They are, as are not.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.129
Shankar Ossorio, one of the many brilliant
persuaders I write about in the book, says

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:11.750
These persuadable voters are people who can
be toggled into a very progressive way of

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.800
looking at the world or a very conservative
way of looking at the world.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.970
To give you an example on immigration, when
the thing in the air.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:27.810
Is imagery of people crossing, migrants crossing
the border in caravans.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:33.370
Those persuadable people can be toggled into
with the right messages, can be toggled into

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.599
a very right wing nationalist.

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:37.780
Fear based.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.180
Close the border.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.310
As you will remember a couple of years ago,
when.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.110
Family separation.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.850
Was the dominant thing we were all seeing
on the news every day.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:55.019
A lot of those same moderate, persuadable
voters were, if you remember, using the phrase,

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:56.689
this is not who we are.

00:03:56.689 --> 00:03:57.819
Right.

00:03:57.819 --> 00:04:01.560
Those same people who are in a different circumstance
might have been toggled into a very right

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.700
wing understanding of the border.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.079
We're in that moment with that set of facts,
that set of stimuli toggled into a very progressive

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:09.360
understanding of the question.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.670
And so this book is about how you speak to
those significant numbers of people in the

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.560
middle who you don't need most of them.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.730
You don't need all of them.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:26.670
But if you can win many of them in a given
moment, in a given election, on a given question,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.830
it can make the difference between very different
futures.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:36.410
Speaker 1: When people often will call in
and ask about persuasion, whether it's at

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:40.729
a dinner table or whether it's in a national
election.

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:46.860
Oftentimes the conversation is different,
and we've talked with people who specialize

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:52.020
in kind of changing beliefs at what we might
call the retail level, which is you build

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.639
a relationship with someone that you personally
know.

00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:58.919
And there's often many conversations and here's
the average number of hours that it can take.

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:01.460
And that's sort of like one bucket.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:09.979
The other bucket is widely held beliefs for
large numbers of people for the purposes of

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:15.780
winning an election and how views can be shifted
in those sort of situations.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:20.520
Can you talk a little bit about with that
second group, which is for the purposes of

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:26.569
winning an election with views that might
include Trump was actually the winner in 2020,

00:05:26.569 --> 00:05:28.440
it was stolen from him.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.389
Or, you know, Democrats want to do abortions
when a baby is already six months out of the

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:35.130
womb or whatever the case may be.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:39.210
What are the techniques that can be used in
those sorts of situations?

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:44.580
Speaker 4: Well, the last the the actual examples
you raised are in a way not examples of arguments,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:45.910
but examples of disinformation.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:46.910
Right.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.870
So there's a chapter in the book that is about
persuasion in the context of disinformation,

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:59.330
which in some ways is a separate, separate
question from traditional, you know, traditional

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.330
political persuasion.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.330
Speaker 1: So.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:02.669
And just to be clear, it's different.

00:06:02.669 --> 00:06:03.669
Why?

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:07.569
Because it's simply based on a misunderstanding
of a fact rather than maybe conflicting morals

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:08.569
or something like that.

00:06:08.569 --> 00:06:09.569
Speaker 4: Exactly.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:20.890
Like if you and I are having an argument about
whether, you know, a fetus is a person deserving

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.720
of legal status and protection from homicide
laws or not.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:25.720
Yeah.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:30.290
That's not an issue of you and I having different
facts or senses of reality.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:32.669
Like the facts are broadly agreed.

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:39.039
It's a, you know, whether a fetus is a person
deserving that protection is a moral argument

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:45.139
that depends on, you know, interpretation
and that and a lot of politics, you know,

00:06:45.139 --> 00:06:53.250
or just like is a 31% marginal top tax rate
or a 39% one going to enhance economic growth

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:55.100
and broadly shared prosperity?

00:06:55.100 --> 00:07:01.820
Again, no one is we're not, you know, in hopefully
in a good version of that conversation, we're

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.130
looking at a common set of facts and historical
data.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:07.350
And when we're interpreted as things are complicated,
right?

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:08.350
Yeah.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:12.750
The examples you cited really are where we
are now in many cases where it's like moon

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:19.100
is made of green cheese territory and you
know, the world is run by a global pedophile

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:23.280
ring run out of a pizza restaurant on Connecticut
Avenue in Washington, D.C., kind of thing,

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:24.280
yeah.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:29.129
Q And on 43 million Americans, by the way,
now believe in the underlying fantasies of

00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:34.530
QAnon, which means 43 million Americans could
be understood to be in a diffused, dispersed,

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.699
you know, virtual cult.

00:07:36.699 --> 00:07:37.699
Yeah.

00:07:37.699 --> 00:07:39.690
So the issue you raise is very important.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:45.110
And I spoke to two different experts for that
chapter about what we do about this problem.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:49.289
How do you persuade in the context of people
having really their own sense of reality that

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:51.690
is non-reality and the truth?

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:56.140
You know, one of them was a cult deprogramming
former cult victim herself who got out of

00:07:56.140 --> 00:08:01.129
the cult and then helps other people get out
of cults one at a time.

00:08:01.129 --> 00:08:02.129
Right.

00:08:02.129 --> 00:08:06.479
And she's now thinking about what do you do
when millions of people are being manipulated

00:08:06.479 --> 00:08:11.410
in cult like ways by leaders like Donald Trump
or into disinformation?

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:16.470
And then I spoke to a cognitive scientist
who works a lot on COVID and I mean a climate

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:20.970
and then now COVID disinformation and trying
to figure out how do you deal with that and

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:25.310
what both of them said from their different
vantage points that really surprised me was

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:31.879
that essentially we may need to treat disinformation
as endemic in the age of the Internet, that

00:08:31.879 --> 00:08:36.849
there's not going to be a I mean, literally
a disinformation artist owns Twitter right

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:37.849
now.

00:08:37.849 --> 00:08:38.849
Yes.

00:08:38.849 --> 00:08:45.360
The idea of the idea of shutting off the spigot
feels, I think to both of them, a little fanciful.

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.610
And what they suggest is sticking with the
kind of public health endemic analogy.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:54.190
If this is endemic, if disinformation is going
to be in the air.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.210
The answer is to inoculate people against
it.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.770
Don't worry, this is not like an actual shot.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:04.860
But a public health approach to disinformation
is what they suggest, which is essentially

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:11.380
an educational an educational shift, where
basically the way, you know, things like sex

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:16.210
ed were once taught to people in their homes,
if at all, or people figured it out behind

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:17.620
the barn or whatever.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:22.420
Now, you know, sex ed came to be thought of
as something that was crucial to any child's

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:23.420
development.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:28.150
The state, the society has an obligation to
have everybody have a common minimum understanding

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:33.330
of their own bodies and sexuality and where
babies come from and and so on and so forth.

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:39.170
Well, I think being able to detect and kind
of interdict these common tactics of cult

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:45.589
manipulation and disinformation may become
viewed as a core part of an education.

00:09:45.589 --> 00:09:50.220
Today, you know, critical thinking is what
like here's a passage of a book.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:53.620
You know, what are the three main ideas and
which one you that's not?

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:55.459
Speaker 1: No, no, no.

00:09:55.459 --> 00:09:56.459
That's quite lacking.

00:09:56.459 --> 00:10:01.839
Speaker 4: That definition of critical thinking
was, you know, not adequate, I think, to,

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:04.310
you know, 15 year olds being up against Rupert
Murdoch.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:09.060
And we need to fortify them and to detect
how and when they're being conned.

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:10.830
Speaker 1: Because you mentioned cults.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:14.610
And we've we've also interviewed experts on
cults and getting people out of called former

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.480
cult members, etc..

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.331
Obviously, the inoculation ahead of time,
preventing people from getting sucked in in

00:10:20.331 --> 00:10:21.331
the first place.

00:10:21.331 --> 00:10:22.331
Absolutely.

00:10:22.331 --> 00:10:23.331
A great thing.

00:10:23.331 --> 00:10:24.331
Important thing.

00:10:24.331 --> 00:10:26.260
Reality is a lot of people are already sucked
in.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:31.940
What methods of persuasion have you seen be
most effective at getting someone out of a

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:32.940
cult?

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:36.960
Speaker 4: So the the interesting thing here
that I learned again from both Diane, Ben

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.730
Scudder and and John Cook, the scientist,
is that what we are?

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:48.300
What we tend to do instinctually when we encounter
those people is as backwards, almost designed

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.389
to not work and drive them further into the
arms of the con.

00:10:51.389 --> 00:10:54.410
What we what we often do is make them feel
stupid.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:59.670
We often try to rebut their points point by
point.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:03.240
Hagel on reality present data.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:07.260
A lot of us are, you know, on the other side
and the reality based community are like interested

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.200
in data and evidence and we kind of lead with
that.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.280
And we don't understand what's going on there.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:19.570
And so what both Diane and John educated me
on is basically they view as someone who has

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:25.980
who has succumbed already, as you say, as
someone who there's kind of two warring forces

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.060
within them.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:30.080
That person and one of them has is badly beating
the other.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:35.839
The first force is the desire to have the
world make facile sense.

00:11:35.839 --> 00:11:37.420
We all have that desire.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:40.070
Every single one of us has that desire.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.210
Yep.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.860
Cults and disinformation prey on that desire.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:44.860
Right.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:45.860
The world is complicated.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:47.440
Globalization is complicated.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:48.490
Covert is complicated.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:49.940
Q And on comes in.

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:51.500
It's actually very simple, right?

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:54.220
It's appealing and it hooks certain people.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:59.820
The second desire that is also in every human
heart is the desire to be no one's fool, the

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:05.330
desire to be no one's dupe, the desire not
to be a mark for the powerful, manipulative

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:06.339
people.

00:12:06.339 --> 00:12:10.510
And what has happened in people who've succumb
to disinformation is that force number one

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:12.089
has really outgunned force.

00:12:12.089 --> 00:12:16.130
Number two, in them, force number two is kind
of lying latent.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:21.889
And so the thing that works is not factual
rebuttal or evidence or making them feel dumb,

00:12:21.889 --> 00:12:33.220
but helping them see that they may be being
used by powerful people who are manipulating

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:37.760
them for the benefit of those powerful people
and not their benefit, helping them see the

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:39.310
ways in which they've been tricked.

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:41.132
To be clear, this is not going to work all
the time.

00:12:41.132 --> 00:12:43.230
It may not work most of the time.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:46.260
But it has shown some results.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:49.090
Diane Ben Scott, who wrote about her own experience
being in the Moonies.

00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:52.910
And, you know, these these the programmers
came and tried to.

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:53.910
Her mother organized.

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:55.040
They tried to come and get her out.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.380
And they were telling her the beliefs about
Christ are not true.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:12:59.380
Here's the truth about this.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:00.380
Here the truth.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:01.380
None of it worked.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:02.380
Well, it worked on her.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:08.260
She was sitting in this room and they start
telling her about brainwashing techniques

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.899
in Mao's Revolutionary China.

00:13:10.899 --> 00:13:11.899
Mm hmm.

00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:19.360
And what was interesting is she didn't have
emotional defense mechanisms around Mao's

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:20.660
Revolutionary China.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:21.660
Right?

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:22.660
Right.

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.220
It was not coming at her straight.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:28.190
It was like exotic a world away.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:29.190
Right.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:32.560
And so maybe she listened a little more easily
than she might have if it had been straight

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:33.560
out.

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.519
Here's why what you're saying about Jesus
is not true.

00:13:36.519 --> 00:13:42.459
And she started hearing the list of Mao's
brainwashing techniques.

00:13:42.459 --> 00:13:45.079
And she just recognized them.

00:13:45.079 --> 00:13:50.829
She just and she started she just kind of
described, I think, the world like crashing

00:13:50.829 --> 00:13:54.029
pieces of glass all around her as a sensation.

00:13:54.029 --> 00:13:55.079
She felt just and.

00:13:55.079 --> 00:13:59.089
And she kept muttering herself like, what
if this was all a lie?

00:13:59.089 --> 00:14:02.730
And so, again, I don't want to I don't want
to overstate how easy this is.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:03.730
Speaker 1: Right.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:04.730
It doesn't always go that well.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:09.759
Speaker 4: But but that I think that's the
dynamic you are aiming for, planting a seed

00:14:09.759 --> 00:14:10.759
of doubt.

00:14:10.759 --> 00:14:14.650
And I would say lastly, this comes up again
and again in the book, not just on this information.

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:20.959
Persuasion often involves trying to displace
what is in people's heads, not trying to replace

00:14:20.959 --> 00:14:26.030
what is in other's heads, and certainly not
being perceived as having an agenda of replacing

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:27.230
what is in their head.

00:14:27.230 --> 00:14:30.580
Simply trying to displace to make people have
questions again.

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:33.880
Speaker 1: So displace rather than replace.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:39.860
Makes me want to talk about the group of canvassers
you spoke to in Arizona and how they were

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:43.699
focused on changing people's minds on on immigration.

00:14:43.699 --> 00:14:45.160
What were they doing?

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:51.269
Speaker 4: This was a you know, of all the
of all the different people I studied and,

00:14:51.269 --> 00:14:55.389
you know, try the different types of persuaders
I wrote about from the scientists to educators

00:14:55.389 --> 00:15:02.290
to activist organizers at these canvassers
in Arizona filled me with so much hope they

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:06.890
were engaged in this experiment called Deep
Canvasing that grew out of the stinging defeat

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:10.960
of gay marriage in California in 2008, on
the night that Barack Obama got elected.

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:15.149
Right know an otherwise an otherwise kind
of glorious night in American history that

00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:21.160
was, you know, that overshadowed this really
awful defeat by referendum in California.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:26.630
In other words, most people's neighbors were
against.

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:29.649
The view of gay people as full human beings.

00:15:29.649 --> 00:15:31.199
That was a they didn't know that.

00:15:31.199 --> 00:15:32.199
They didn't know that.

00:15:32.199 --> 00:15:34.220
That was the majority view.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.720
The vote lost L.A. County.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:41.100
Gay marriage lost L.A. County.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:45.759
You know what we would think of as a reasonably
decent place to be gay in the United States.

00:15:45.759 --> 00:15:49.810
Gay marriage lost L.A. County in 2008.

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:55.290
And so these gay rights activists in the L.A.
LGBT Center and others were just in a funk.

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:59.380
And, you know, one of them described walking
around the grocery store and you're, like

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:01.620
standing in the produce aisle being like.

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:05.019
Like, Does this person next to me want to
exterminate me?

00:16:05.019 --> 00:16:09.560
Like, does this person think I'm not a person
like who you know, it's not Mitch McConnell

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:13.329
and like 2000 miles away, it's not people
in the Central Valley.

00:16:13.329 --> 00:16:18.120
Like, I am surrounded by people who do not
think I'm a human being.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:20.819
And it you know, again, this is not for the
faint of heart.

00:16:20.819 --> 00:16:21.819
It's not for most people.

00:16:21.819 --> 00:16:28.060
But a certain subset of these gay activists
in L.A. in that time were like, I want to

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:31.490
go to knock on people's doors and find out
why they hate me.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:36.110
Again, you may be listening to this thinking,
who the hell would want to do that?

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:37.110
Yeah.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:39.100
And, like, totally great to stay at home and
not do that.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.190
Yes, but these people wanted to go do that.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:42.329
They wanted to know.

00:16:42.329 --> 00:16:43.350
They actually had questions.

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:44.350
Right.

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:47.459
They didn't they couldn't understand why people
couldn't understand them.

00:16:47.459 --> 00:16:48.459
And so they went.

00:16:48.459 --> 00:16:53.529
And what started kind of informally, helter
skelter talking to people listening has since

00:16:53.529 --> 00:16:57.889
developed into a method that is remarkable
that I witnessed at it's kind of, you know,

00:16:57.889 --> 00:17:03.170
maturity in in Arizona, where these canvassers
go door to door.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:07.199
They don't do 32nd conversations about have
you found your polling place or do you have

00:17:07.199 --> 00:17:08.199
a flier?

00:17:08.199 --> 00:17:13.839
They spend 30 to 40 minutes lovingly, patiently,
strategically talking people through their

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:21.650
views, their deepest prejudices, as well as
values that might conflict with their prejudices.

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:24.610
They don't they again, displace, not replace.

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:29.030
They don't come in there and say, well, you
say this about immigrants being lazy, but

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:34.250
do you know, in fact, David, that 25% of immigrants
work 900 hours?

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:36.299
That's what they write.

00:17:36.299 --> 00:17:37.740
What they look for is.

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:39.289
So you said immigrants are lazy.

00:17:39.289 --> 00:17:41.419
Do you know any immigrants personally?

00:17:41.419 --> 00:17:42.490
Oh, well, yes.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:43.900
I had my gardener.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:46.130
Manuel is Manuel is Manuel lazy?

00:17:46.130 --> 00:17:50.289
I know Manuel works like harder than most
people I know.

00:17:50.289 --> 00:17:52.980
And is he the only one whose family is, like,
better?

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:54.669
No, actually, his kids are so hardworking.

00:17:54.669 --> 00:17:59.070
They're like in college, but they're also
working two jobs.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:03.880
And at some point you just say to them, how
is the thing you said at the beginning sitting

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:05.400
with you as you now?

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:11.590
Tell me about Manuel and you just let it be
quite the great little silence there for.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:12.590
Yeah.

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:13.970
And people.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:17.560
You know, this may sound either believable
to you if you're listening to this or not

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:18.560
believable.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:21.640
But not everyone has probably thought about
these issues as much as you and I might have.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:25.370
And people listening to a podcast about these
issues might have people stand there in the

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.600
door and they're like.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:28.600
It's true.

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.290
A lot of the immigrants I know are actually
the opposite of lazy.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:32.290
Huh.

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:34.360
I wonder why I always go around thinking they're
lazy.

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:35.360
Yeah, right.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:36.360
That happens.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:37.360
That's real.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:40.620
It's true that people will say a thing about
trans people.

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:43.210
And then I saw this powerful video of one
of these canvasses.

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:45.890
A black woman in California.

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:52.090
Talking about her own trans niece and basically,
like ragging on her own trans niece and then

00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:53.310
the canvassers like.

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:57.780
Have you ever been discriminated against or
treated badly because of things you couldn't

00:18:57.780 --> 00:18:58.780
control?

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:01.880
Interestingly, as a black woman in America,
she did not connect it to being black.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:03.320
She did not connect to being a woman.

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:09.530
She connected it to being like moving from
another city and working in a job in L.A.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:13.169
and everyone in her job in L.A. being like,
you should you should go back to where you

00:19:13.169 --> 00:19:14.190
came from.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.309
Yeah, that was just like not from being around
there.

00:19:17.309 --> 00:19:18.360
And she was like, that sucked.

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:23.340
I just went to work every day and it just
sucked to like, I can't help that I lived

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:24.340
in another city.

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:25.340
Right.

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:30.190
And then he is like, and how did that feel
to be just seen and treated that way?

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:31.190
And and you see this.

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:34.190
You see it like a wave.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:39.260
Crash on this woman's face like, oh, I am
to my knees.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:42.080
What those people were to me right now.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:46.929
And so I think this work of deep canvasing,
like so much of the work of persuasion, depends

00:19:46.929 --> 00:19:54.990
on a kind of faith that many people, if not
most, can change, can move.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:20:00.210
Think what they think, in part because of
what they've seen, what they have not seen,

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:03.030
where they've been, where they've not been.

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:07.760
And that exposure and conversation and listening
and empathy can get us there.

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:13.159
This is not an argument to go around knocking
on dangerous doors and, you know, hoping to

00:20:13.159 --> 00:20:14.960
be respected if you're a marginalized person.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:20.350
This is a book about some people who choose
to do this work out of their own volition

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.809
because they believe that people can change.

00:20:22.809 --> 00:20:29.090
Speaker 1: The book is The Persuaders at the
Frontlines of the Fight for Hearts, Minds

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:30.330
and Democracy.

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:32.400
We've been speaking with the book's author.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:36.510
Anand Giridharadas, always great having you
on.

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:38.320
And really great, great book.

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:39.320
Very much enjoyed it.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:41.500
And we're going to link to it and encourage
our audience to pick it up.

00:20:41.500 --> 00:20:43.221
Speaker 4: Well, thank you so much for having
me.

00:20:43.221 --> 00:20:43.490
It's good to be back.

